{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Fathers shall not be put to death for children.</b> Even for the crime of rebellion against the kingdom of Israel — for it was the practice of the early kings to kill also their children, so that they would not rise up as adversaries to the kingdom, as in: הָכִינוּ לְבָנָיו מַטְבֵּחַ בַּעֲוֹן אֲבוֹתָם, בַּל יָקֻמוּ וְיָרְשׁוּ אָרֶץ וּמָלְאוּ פְנֵי תֵבֵל עָרִים (Isaiah 14:21). The meaning of שׂוֹנְאִים is like וַיְהִי עָרֶךְ (1 Samuel 28:17). In any case, the Torah forbade the kings of Israel to kill one for the sake of the other, in HaShem's compassion upon His people. This Amaziah king of Judah fulfilled, as it says: וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר חָזְקָה הַמַּמְלָכָה בְּיָדוֹ וַיַּהֲרֹג אֶת עֲבָדָיו הַמַּכִּים אֶת הַמֶּלֶךְ אָבִיו, וְאֶת בְּנֵי הַמַּכִּים לֹא הֵמִית, כַּכָּתוּב בַּתּוֹרָה בְּסֵפֶר מֹשֶׁה אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ לֵאמֹר לֹא יוּמְתוּ אָבוֹת עַל בָּנִים וּבָנִים לֹא יוּמְתוּ עַל אָבוֹת (2 Chronicles 25:3–4)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The parenthetical מציעא appears in the source text; its meaning and function are unclear and it is left untranslated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:22:10.890420852Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}